---
import StoryLayout from '../layouts/StoryLayout.astro';
import StoryArt from '../components/StoryArt.astro';
---
<StoryLayout title="Try Froglet · Public beta" description="Run your program on a public Froglet provider, query a synthetic catalog, and verify the signed result. No installation or invitation required." path="/services/">
<div class="story-wrap">
  <section class="story-hero story-hero--compact">
    <div class="story-hero__copy">
      <span class="story-kicker">Try it yourself · Public beta</span>
      <h1>A question goes out.<br /><span class="story-accent">A result comes back.</span></h1>
      <p class="story-lede">Send a small program to Bob’s provider. Get its result and check the signed exchange in your browser.</p>
      <div class="story-needs"><ul aria-label="Demo access"><li>No installation</li><li>No invitation</li><li>Free bounded jobs</li></ul></div>
      <div class="story-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#try-it">Run a job now</a><a class="btn secondary" href="/open-source/#playground">Explore the local playground</a></div>
      <p class="story-note">The hosted demo uses a real Froglet node. The separate playground runs both sides locally in your browser.</p>
    </div>
    <StoryArt name="06-discovery" eager alt="Alice exploring tools and data from her laptop." />
  </section>

  <section class="story-section live-demo" id="try-it" aria-labelledby="try-title" data-live-demo>
    <div class="story-heading"><span class="story-kicker">Your browser is Alice. The provider is Bob.</span><h2 id="try-title">Change the input.<br />Run the work.</h2><p>Your browser compiles the program and signs the request. Bob’s native runtime executes it with fuel, memory and time limits. Your browser checks his receipt against the returned result.</p></div>
    <div class="story-grid story-grid--two">
      <article class="story-status live-demo-card" aria-labelledby="program-title">
        <span class="story-kicker">Bring a program</span><h3 id="program-title">Add and multiply</h3>
        <p>Try new numbers. You can also change the program before sending it.</p>
        <label for="program-input">JSON input</label>
        <textarea id="program-input" data-program-input rows="4" maxlength="131072" spellcheck="false">{'{"a": 6, "b": 7}'}</textarea>
        <details><summary>Change the program</summary><p>This AssemblyScript function compiles in your browser. Bob runs the compiled WebAssembly program. Try replacing <code>a + b</code> with <code>a * a + b</code>.</p><label for="program-source">Program source</label><textarea id="program-source" data-program-source rows="14" maxlength="65536" spellcheck="false"></textarea><button type="button" class="btn secondary" data-reset-program>Restore example</button></details>
        <button type="button" class="btn primary" data-run-program disabled>Run on Bob</button>
        <p class="story-note">Use sample data. Your compiled program and input go to the demo server, which keeps job records.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="story-status live-demo-card" aria-labelledby="catalog-title">
        <span class="story-kicker">Query selected data</span><h3 id="catalog-title">Look up a term</h3>
        <p>Query a tiny catalog of invented terminology. The blood label has two targets, so you can inspect the ambiguity.</p>
        <label for="query-term">Which rows?</label>
        <select id="query-term" data-query-term><option value="">All five rows</option><option value="DEMO:sample.plasma">Plasma</option><option value="DEMO:sample.serum">Serum</option><option value="DEMO:sample.blood">Blood</option><option value="DEMO:sample.saliva">Saliva — no mapping</option></select>
        <button type="button" class="btn primary" data-run-query disabled>Query Bob’s catalog</button>
        <div class="live-demo-failure"><h4>What if a program never stops?</h4><p>Run an infinite loop. Bob stops it within the execution budget and signs a failure receipt.</p><button type="button" class="btn secondary" data-run-failure disabled>Try the budget limit</button></div>
      </article>
    </div>
    <section class="live-demo-result" aria-labelledby="result-title">
      <span class="story-kicker">Returned by the provider</span><h3 id="result-title">Your result</h3>
      <p class="live-demo-status" data-job-status role="status" aria-live="polite">Connecting to the public demo…</p>
      <pre data-job-result aria-label="Returned JSON result"></pre>
      <p class="story-note live-demo-commitment" data-program-commitment></p>
      <div class="story-actions"><button type="button" class="btn secondary" data-download-receipt disabled>Download signed exchange</button><button type="button" class="btn secondary" data-retry-job disabled>Retry this job</button></div>
      <details data-exchange-export hidden><summary>Inspect or copy the signed exchange</summary><p>This JSON can also be pasted into the <a href="/verify-receipt/">standalone record verifier</a>. It contains public signed records, not a private signing key.</p><label for="exchange-json">Signed exchange JSON</label><textarea id="exchange-json" data-exchange-json rows="12" readonly spellcheck="false"></textarea></details>
      <p class="story-note" data-recovery-note>The request stays in this browser session so an interrupted job can be recovered.</p>
      <details><summary>Discard a previous local reference</summary><p>An already submitted job may still have completed. Discarding its reference does not cancel it or replenish the shared allowance. A new run requests new work.</p><button type="button" class="btn secondary" data-discard-job>Discard local reference</button></details>
    </section>
    <p class="story-note" data-demo-capacity>Each program has at most 2 seconds, 8 MiB of memory and 50 million fuel. The public beta has a finite shared allowance. Calls may be refused when capacity is busy or spent.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section"><div class="story-heading"><span class="story-kicker">What you are trying</span><h2>A supplied program.<br />A bounded host.</h2><p>The program can change for one question. The host keeps its admission rules, resource limits and signed records.</p></div>
    <div class="story-scroll"><table class="story-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">You can try</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Your program and JSON input</th><td>The browser compiles the function. The remote node runs its exact bytes and reports their hash.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">A selected terminology query</th><td>The provider returns only the published synthetic snapshot and selected fields.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">An infinite loop</th><td>The execution budget stops it. You can inspect the signed failure record.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">A network interruption</th><td>Retry the saved job. Recovering an accepted result does not request another deal.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">The signed evidence</th><td>The browser verifies the provider’s commitments and the result hash. Signatures do not establish scientific truth or physical execution.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="story-note">This beta demonstrates free Wasm jobs and a synthetic catalog. Paid operation, GPU work, arbitrary containers and sustained production capacity are separate work.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="story-closing"><h2>Use your own agent next.</h2><p>The same native Froglet runtime is available for MCP clients and HTTP integrations. Providers choose their own access policy; invitation-only hosting is an option, not a requirement of Froglet.</p><div class="story-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/learn/agent-interoperability/#native-compute-and-recovery">Native compute guide</a><a class="btn secondary" href="/verify-receipt/">Explore record verification</a></div></section>
</div>
</StoryLayout>
<script>
  import { startLiveServices } from '../scripts/live-services-ui';
  startLiveServices();
</script>
<style>
  .live-demo-card { min-width:0; }
  .live-demo label { display:block; margin:20px 0 8px; font-weight:600; color:var(--fg1); }
  .live-demo textarea,.live-demo select { box-sizing:border-box; width:100%; border:1px solid var(--border-strong); border-radius:6px; padding:14px; background:var(--copy-bg); color:var(--copy-text); font:14px/1.6 var(--font-mono); }
  .live-demo textarea { resize:vertical; }
  .live-demo details { margin-block:20px; }
  .live-demo summary { cursor:pointer; color:var(--fg1); font-weight:600; }
  .live-demo details p { margin-top:14px; }
  .live-demo .btn { margin-top:18px; cursor:pointer; }
  .live-demo .btn:disabled { opacity:.5; cursor:not-allowed; }
  .live-demo-failure { margin-top:42px; padding-top:24px; border-top:1px solid var(--border-strong); }
  .live-demo-failure h4 { color:var(--fg1); font-size:20px; }
  .live-demo-result { margin-top:46px; padding-top:30px; border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong); }
  .live-demo-status { white-space:pre-wrap; color:var(--fg1); }
  .live-demo-result pre { min-height:100px; overflow:auto; padding:22px; border:1px solid var(--border-strong); border-radius:6px; background:var(--copy-bg); color:var(--copy-text); font:15px/1.6 var(--font-mono); }
  .live-demo-commitment { overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
  @media(max-width:420px) { .live-demo-card { padding:22px; } .live-demo-result pre { padding:14px; } }
</style>
